# WHAT THE COMPOSER SAYS EACH PART'S REGIME IS, BESIDE WHAT THAT PART'S OWN FILING DECLARES. One
# row per resolved part carrying a regime handle, with both claims and whether they agree.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ TWO PARTIES, TWO CLAIMS ABOUT ONE THING, AND THE GRAMMAR CANNOT ASK WHETHER THEY AGREE.
#    `asrt:part/asrt:layer/asrt:regime` is a handle into the composition's OWN declarations, and
#    XSD 1.0 checks that it resolves: `compositionRegimeId` is the key, `partRegime` the keyref.
#    That is the strongest thing a grammar can do here and it stops at the document edge, because
#    an identity constraint is scoped to one document and the schema's own annotation says so.
#    Whether the composer's claim matches what the OTHER filing declares about itself is exactly
#    what falls to a rule.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE FRAMEWORK IS COMPARED AS A PAIR AND NEVER AS A CODE. `pm:Regime`'s annotation is
#    emphatic: SAF-T PT publishes `S · M · N · O`, IES AnexoASNC publishes
#    `NIC · NCRF · NCRF-PE · NC-ME`, the lists do not line up, and "a code without its authority
#    is genuinely ambiguous rather than merely unattributed". So the join is on taxonomy AND
#    value. Comparing values alone would call `NCRF-PE` and `S` different frameworks and `M` and
#    `M` the same one, both wrongly.
#
# ⛔ A PART FILING MAY DECLARE MORE THAN ONE REGIME AND `refutation` DOES, so agreement is
#   MEMBERSHIP and not equality: the composer's framework must appear among the frameworks that
#   filing declares. Equality would accuse a document for declaring a second authority's coding,
#   which is the very thing the tall `regime` table exists to permit.
#
# ⚠️ `agrees` IS NULL WHERE EITHER SIDE IS TYPED ABSENT, not false. A fixture reporting under no
#   framework and a composer who said `notApplicable` have not disagreed; the question does not
#   arise. `checks/part_regime_disagrees.sqlc` keeps that out of its population rather than
#   reading it as a pass.
-- pm.part's regime handle resolved into pm.composition_regime, against the part filing's own pm.regime.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, p.part_regime,
       cr.framework_taxonomy AS composer_taxonomy,
       cr.framework_value    AS composer_value,
       cr.framework_absent   AS composer_absent,
       (cr.framework_taxonomy IS NOT NULL AND EXISTS (
            SELECT 1 FROM pm.regime r
            WHERE r.filing = p.part_filing
              AND r.framework_taxonomy = cr.framework_taxonomy
              AND r.framework_value    = cr.framework_value))            AS agrees,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM pm.regime r WHERE r.filing = p.part_filing
          AND r.framework_taxonomy IS NOT NULL)                          AS frameworks_the_filing_states
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
JOIN pm.composition_regime cr
  ON cr.composition = p.composition AND cr.id = p.part_regime
